---
title: Crispy chicken thighs
description: Lesson 1.1. Rendering fat, why this pan starts cold, and what heat does to skin and muscle.
servings: 2
time: 40 minutes
tags: [cooking-course, heat-in-a-pan]
lesson: "1.1"
technique: rendering
status: draft
---

> Technique: rendering. Chicken skin sits on a layer of fat. Start it in
> a cold pan and bring the heat up slowly, and that fat melts out while
> the skin turns thin, golden and crisp.
-- source: Morgan Bolling, "Why It Can Pay to NOT Preheat Your Skillet", America's Test Kitchen, 2022-05-18, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/5513-why-it-can-pay-to-not-preheat-your-skillet

Pat @chicken thighs{4}(bone-in, skin-on) dry on both sides with kitchen paper. Season both sides with @salt{1%tsp} and @black pepper{}.

Lay the thighs skin side down in a cold #large frying pan{} with no oil. Set the pan over medium-high heat and leave them alone for ~{10%minutes}, until a pool of fat has gathered, the skin is deep gold, and a thigh lifts off the pan without sticking.

> Why: you added no oil, and the pan is now full of it. That is the
> fat from under the skin, and the skin has been frying in it.

> Science: three things happen to that skin. Its fat melts out, which
> is rendering, a physical change. Its water boils off; water cannot
> rise above 100 °C until it has turned to vapour, so wet skin stays
> near that temperature. Browning is the Maillard reaction, sugars
> reacting with amino acids, and it runs fast only above that.
> Look up: fat rendering, latent heat of vaporization, Maillard
> reaction.
-- source: Harold McGee, On Food and Cooking, 2nd ed., Scribner, 2004, sections "The Maillard Reactions" and "High Temperatures and Dry Cooking Methods", https://app.ckbk.com/reference/food00011c14s001e001se003/high-temperatures-and-dry-cooking-methods

> If it goes wrong: if the skin is getting dark before much fat has
> gathered in the pan, turn the heat down.

Turn the thighs over, lower the heat to medium, and cook for ~{15%minutes} more, until the thickest part of the meat, away from the bone, reads at least 74 °C on a #thermometer{}.
-- source: USDA Food Safety and Inspection Service, "Safe Minimum Internal Temperature Chart", last updated 2025-04-14: all poultry 165 °F (73.9 °C), https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/food-safety-basics/safe-temperature-chart

> Safety: 74 °C is the safe minimum for all poultry, read before the
> meat leaves the heat.

> Science: 74 °C is the safety line; texture is a separate question.
> Muscle proteins set in overlapping bands: myosin at about 40 to
> 60 °C, actin at about 70 to 80 °C. Connective tissue is collagen,
> which shrinks at about 62 to 68 °C and dissolves into gelatin in
> quantity only near 80 °C. America's Test Kitchen cooks thighs to at
> least 175 °F, which is 79 °C. Look up: protein denaturation, myosin,
> actin, collagen, gelatin.
-- source: Jiang et al., "Molecular mechanisms underlying sensory and chemical changes in muscle foods induced by sous-vide cooking: a review", Foods 14(17):2967, 2025, https://doi.org/10.3390/foods14172967 (ranges for muscle foods in general; an older calorimetry study of beef puts actin lower, at 66 to 73 °C) ; the America's Test Kitchen article cited above says "at least 175 degrees"

Rest the thighs on a plate, skin side up and uncovered, for ~{5%minutes} before serving.

> Next time, without the recipe: skin-on chicken, dried well, skin down
> in a cold pan with no oil, medium-high heat, and patience until the
> skin lets go. Then turn it and cook it through to 74 °C.
